+### Private startup protocol
+
+Phase 005 adds fixed named product callbacks for registration, reservation, observation, release, rotation and retirement. They resolve a persisted command-delivery intent, seed-builder intent or explicitly approved migration. There is no public intent-minting or launch endpoint. Live trusted eligibility and accounting cutover remain closed. Approved isolated fixtures exercise this protocol without establishing deployment or HTTPS-interception evidence.
+
+Product assigns identities after runtime routing and guards the D1 batch against fresh ownership, generation, incarnation, namespace and retirement state. Paired startup requires both brain and execution assignments and an atomic reservation. Builders receive only a seed Sandbox and an execution slot. Readiness and execution-admission writes require the corresponding paired assignment and current reservation records, not legacy scalar identity fields alone.
+
+The runtime's registered launch path reserves capacity before starting either container. It persists a launch attempt, installs incarnation-bound outbound parameters, rechecks product authority and starts with no product values or platform credentials in the launch environment. An uncertain start acknowledgment remains unresolved. Capacity observation matches the registered launch and platform state; expiry or isolation never frees an occupied slot. Only matching termination does. An approved rotation or retirement closes admission records while retaining outcomes and occupied reservations for reconciliation. Retirement adds a permanent tombstone.
+
+`runtime_capacity_policy` stays in legacy accounting mode by default. Local fixtures may select unified accounting. Migration approval, paired baselines, real Pi lifecycle and public activation still belong to their later implementation phases.
+
 ## Lifecycle
 
 Project status is `provisioning`, `ready`, `failed`, or `deleting`. The `deleting` status blocks new work while authority and storage cleanup run.
